# Agience Mantle — configuration template. Copy to `.env`.
#
# ═══ The store ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The two the Quick Start treats as primary, and the only two set outright below.

# The one SQLite file that IS the store. The schema is created on open; there is no database
# process to provision. Relative paths resolve against the working directory. Unset, the store
# defaults to `<AGIENCE_BASE_DIR>/.data/mantle-lattice.db` — derived from the install root like
# KEYS_DIR and MANTLE_SSE_DIR below, so it does not follow the directory you started from.
MANTLE_LATTICE_PATH=./.data/mantle.db

# Where key material lives. AN EMPTY DIRECTORY DOES NOT BOOT: the lifespan loads an encryption key
# and an inbound-nonce secret from files and refuses to invent them — a store that generated its
# own encryption key at startup would have re-keyed every secret it holds. Populate a throwaway
# development set with `mantle-init-keys --keys-dir <dir>`.
#
# ⚠ GIVE EVERY INSTALL ITS OWN DIRECTORY. Four of the six files here carry no component in their
# name — `encryption.key`, `inbound_nonce.secret`, `instance.uuid`, `authority.manifest.json` are
# what EVERY Agience component's init writes. Two installs sharing one directory overwrite each
# other, and the damage is one-sided: a replaced signing key costs a restart, but a replaced
# `encryption.key` makes every secret and platform setting sealed under it permanently unreadable.
# So namespace the leaf rather than pointing several components at a common parent:
#
#     ~/.agience/_secrets/mantle      ← this node
#     ~/.agience/_secrets/origin      ← a different component, its own directory
#
# `mantle-init-keys` writes a `keyset.owner` marker and refuses to write into a directory another
# install owns — `--force` does not override that, because overwriting a component that is not
# running is not a decision this command line can consent to. `mantle-init-keys --verify` checks
# an existing keyset is intact and reports any shared file that was replaced after it was written,
# which is what a collision looks like from this side.
KEYS_DIR=./.data/keys

# Where the encrypted search index lives. Defaults to `<AGIENCE_BASE_DIR>/.data/mantle-sse`, which
# is derived from the install root and NOT from MANTLE_LATTICE_PATH.
#
# ⚠ THE STORE PATH AND THE INDEX PATH ARE SET SEPARATELY, AND ISOLATING A NODE TAKES BOTH. Pointing
# MANTLE_LATTICE_PATH at a second database does not give that database a second index: two nodes in
# one checkout, each with its own lattice, share one index directory unless MANTLE_SSE_DIR is also
# set. Both come up healthy while answering searches from each other's postings. Treat these four
# as one set per node:
#
#     MANTLE_LATTICE_PATH=./node-a.db       ← the store
#     MANTLE_SSE_DIR=./.data/node-a-sse     ← its lexical index
#     MANTLE_CELL_DIR=./.data/node-a-cells  ← its semantic index
#     KEYS_DIR=./.data/node-a-keys          ← its key material
#
# An unusable directory turns that arm OFF rather than falling back to a shared one.
#MANTLE_SSE_DIR=

# Where the encrypted vector cells live. Defaults to `<AGIENCE_BASE_DIR>/.data/mantle-cells`, and
# is the semantic arm's exact counterpart of MANTLE_SSE_DIR above — same derivation, same isolation
# rule, same refusal on an unusable directory.
#MANTLE_CELL_DIR=

# ⚠ NOTHING IN THIS SECTION TURNS THE SEMANTIC ARM ON. MANTLE_CELL_DIR above, and
# MANTLE_CELL_STORE and MANTLE_SEARCH_CUT below, decide WHERE the vector arm writes and where
# its results stop. Whether it runs at all is decided by one thing none of them control: a seeded
# AnchorSet, the shared coordinate system every vector is routed against. A fresh node has none,
# and MANTLE DOES NOT DERIVE, GROW, RECONCILE OR CROSSWALK ONE — anchors fitted to a local corpus
# mint region ids no peer computes, and anchors are vectors, which the no-models rule says this
# process never produces. So on a node nobody has seeded:
#
#     an artifact write        → indexed LEXICALLY; the vector arm returns `skipped` and warns
#     POST /artifacts/recall   → narrows on the query's terms, then answers most-recently-
#                                updated first: `ordering: "recency"`, `score: null` per hit
#     the same call + `vector` → 400, naming this state and both ways out of it. A vector needs
#                                a space to be placed in and this node ranks in none, so the
#                                alternative was a 200 carrying the caller's whole visible
#                                corpus in recency order — a body indistinguishable from the
#                                line above it. The write above is still accepted: a stored
#                                vector is provenance, and seeding later makes it good.
#
# and the first two are a healthy-looking node. The whole procedure is two commands and one
# rule:
#
#     python -m mantle.system.manage_anchors --action inspect                 which state am I in
#     python -m mantle.system.manage_anchors --action load --path anchors.json  seed the set
#
#     then send every query vector with space_id = the set's model_id and the set's dim.
#     Any other space is a 400 naming both. One node serves one space.
#
# `anchors.json` is the single-file JSON `AnchorSet.save` writes — the one format, and the one
# way in. There is no setting here that substitutes for that step, and the README's "Semantic
# recall is inert until you seed an AnchorSet" is the same procedure at length.

# Which backend each arm's index lives on: `auto` (default), `s3`, or `file`. `auto` is S3 where
# edge object storage is configured and the local directory where it is not, so a node with no
# bucket has somewhere to put BOTH indexes — that is a claim about storage, and it is the only
# thing these settings claim. `s3` never falls back — an operator who pinned object storage is
# stating a local index is not acceptable.
#MANTLE_SSE_STORE=auto
#MANTLE_CELL_STORE=auto

# Where the semantic arm's result set STOPS: `beacon` (default) or `none`. The beacon cut reads the
# break off the candidate set's own spectrum — Tracy-Widom signal rank over a query-relative screen,
# split at the largest relative gap — so the result count follows the data instead of a constant.
# `none` returns the whole scored horizon and leaves the caller's page size as the only bound.
# Both values shape a set the semantic arm produced; with no AnchorSet there is no such set and
# this setting has nothing to act on.
#MANTLE_SEARCH_CUT=beacon

# The root every derived default hangs off — KEYS_DIR, MANTLE_LATTICE_PATH, MANTLE_SSE_DIR,
# MANTLE_CELL_DIR and the embeddings cache all default to `<AGIENCE_BASE_DIR>/.data/…`. Set by the
# container image; set by hand when a node must not depend on where it was started from.
#
# Unset, it is derived, and the derivation asks ONE question: is the directory holding the `mantle`
# package a tree this node may write in?
#
#   · a CHECKOUT (and a PEP 660 editable install, which is the same tree seen through a finder)
#     → the repo root, so `.data/` sits beside `src/` where a developer expects it;
#   · a WHEEL INSTALL, a ZIP IMPORT or a FROZEN BUILD → the working directory the node was
#     STARTED in. None of those own a writable tree: site-packages belongs to pip and the next
#     `pip install --upgrade` rewrites it, a zip has no directory behind it at all, and a frozen
#     bundle is unpacked to a temp directory that is DELETED on exit.
#
# "Is this an install directory" is asked of the interpreter (`sysconfig` purelib/platlib, `site`'s
# own lists) rather than guessed from the name, because there is no one name: `site-packages` on a
# venv, `dist-packages` on Debian, neither under `pip install --target`.
#
# ⚠ THE WORKING-DIRECTORY FALLBACK IS A FLOOR, NOT A PLAN. A node started from a different
# directory derives a different root and opens an empty index — the failure MANTLE_LATTICE_PATH and
# MANTLE_SSE_DIR both warn about. It is the right floor (a node started in the wrong place is one
# `cd` from being right; a node whose indexes were deleted by a package upgrade is not), but on an
# installed node SAY IT HERE and stop deriving.
#AGIENCE_BASE_DIR=

# The name this node stamps on the rows it originates — the `origin` column of every vertex and
# edge, and what mesh sync uses to tell one writer's rows from another's. Defaults to `mantle`,
# which is correct for a node that peers with nobody. ⚠ TWO NODES THAT SYNC MUST NOT SHARE ONE
# NAME: give each its own before they are peered, not after, because the rows already written
# carry the name they were written under. The legacy `EMBER_NODE_ID` is still read as a fallback.
#MANTLE_ORIGIN=mantle


# ═══ Service URIs ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# This node's own public address — the `resource` in its RFC 9728 metadata, and what the built-in
# browser client declares as its `redirect_uri`. Declared server-side, never computed in the
# browser.
#
# ⚠ UNCOMMENTING THIS LINE IS ITSELF THE SETTING, even at the default value. What both readers ask
# is whether an operator SAID where this node answers, not what the value happens to be: unset, the
# `http://localhost:8081` below is a developer default and the browser page refuses rather than
# handing an IdP a redirect to localhost from a production host. Set — to anything non-empty,
# including exactly this string — it is a statement, and a node that really does answer on
# localhost can make it. Same rule as AUTHORITY_ISSUER/ORIGIN_URI below.
#MANTLE_URI=http://localhost:8081

# The identity service, when there is one. Mantle does not require it at runtime: a sovereign node
# resolves its operator locally and verifies tokens from an external IdP. Also the fallback source
# for AUTHORITY_ISSUER below.
#
# ⚠ A SOVEREIGN NODE SHOULD SET THIS EMPTY RATHER THAN LEAVE IT UNSET. The default below is a
# live-looking address, and UNSET TAKES IT — so a node that simply never mentions Origin spends
# part of every boot connecting to a peer it does not have. An EMPTY value is a node stating it
# has no Origin, and is honoured as such: no default is substituted and no call is made.
#
# What the probe actually costs, MEASURED on a node with nothing listening on 8080: boot resolves
# the operator from three callers, and one connect attempt answers for all three — a peer that did
# not respond is remembered for 60s, so the second and third callers make no call. That one probe
# is bounded by `clients/origin_client._CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` (0.5s), and the bound is for the
# WHOLE probe rather than per DNS record: `localhost` resolves to both ::1 and 127.0.0.1, and the
# budget is split across them instead of being spent twice. Setting this empty removes the 0.5s
# outright; the remaining boot is unchanged.
#
# The default stays an address because it is also the fallback for AUTHORITY_ISSUER above, and
# because a host-side Mantle in a full-platform dev checkout reaches Origin there without config.
# Neither of those is a reason for a node with no Origin to pay for one — hence the line above.
#
# Also settable from the platform store (`branding.origin_uri`).
#ORIGIN_URI=http://localhost:8080

# The BROWSER APP a human is sent to — the Vite front end `grant_service.build_claim_url` builds
# a claim link against. Its own port, deliberately: the facet (:5173), Origin the identity service
# (:8080) and Mantle itself (:8081) are three different things, and a default pointing at another's
# port makes an unconfigured node name the wrong service. Also settable from the platform store
# (`branding.facet_uri`).
#FACET_URI=http://localhost:5173


# ═══ Identity and trust ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# What a user token must carry as BOTH `iss` and `aud`. Scheme+host+port only — any path is
# stripped, because a path prefix must never appear in those claims. Defaults to ORIGIN_URI.
#AUTHORITY_ISSUER=

# External OIDC IdPs this node verifies directly. A JSON array; each entry is
# {"issuer", "audience", "jwks_uri" | "jwks", "namespace"?, "role"?}. Empty means platform-only.
# This is the seam that makes auth IdP-agnostic: signature, `iss` and `aud` are checked locally
# against the issuer's JWKS, and an external user's id is COMPUTED as uuid5(namespace, (iss, sub)),
# so no identity service need be reachable.
#AGIENCE_TRUSTED_ISSUERS=

# The built-in browser client's OAuth registration — and the WHOLE of the sign-in configuration.
# There is no setting naming an external application to send a human to: the page does not link out
# to a sign-in, it runs one, against the issuer's own discovery document with this node's own
# `redirect_uri`. (`MANTLE_SIGNIN_URI` was that setting. Its only reader was a landing page
# `ui/browse_page.py` superseded, so it accepted a value and did nothing; it is gone rather than
# rewired.) Authorization Code + PKCE, a PUBLIC client.
# There is no secret here and there must not be one: anything shipped to a browser is not a
# secret, and PKCE is what replaces it.
#MANTLE_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
#MANTLE_OIDC_SCOPE=openid profile email

# The Mantle user id holding platform admin. For an operator arriving through an external IdP that
# is the namespaced uuid5(tenant, sub) id, not the raw `sub`. Consulted after this node's own
# `platform.operator_id` setting and before Origin's fallback.
#AGIENCE_OPERATOR_ID=


# ═══ Settings that also live in the platform-settings store ══════════════════
# Each of these is loaded from the lattice at boot and MAY be overridden here: `config.py`'s
# `_SETTING_MAP` gives every one an environment name and checks it first, so an operator's `.env`
# outranks what is stored. The value shown is the in-code default used before the store is read.

# The content edge (S3 / MinIO / any bucket). Needed only to mirror content off-box — a node whose
# working set is local answers with the backing unreachable, by design.
#CONTENT_URI=http://localhost:9000
#CONTENT_BUCKET=agience-content

# Presigned-URL lifetimes, in seconds.
#CONTENT_DOWNLOAD_URL_EXPIRY=300
#CONTENT_UPLOAD_URL_EXPIRY=900
#CONTENT_MULTIPART_PART_URL_EXPIRY=300

# Ingest concurrency. These threads contend on one SQLite write lock, so past a point more of them
# buys nothing — measure before raising it.
#INDEX_QUEUE_MAX_WORKERS=16

# Root log level.
#BACKEND_LOG_LEVEL=info

# Comma-separated collection slugs treated as seed inboxes.
#SEED_COLLECTION_SLUGS=agience-inbox-seeds


# ═══ Content storage: the EDGE tier and the DURABLE tier ═════════════════════
# Read by `services/content_service.py` at IMPORT, not through `config.py`, so nothing in the
# platform store overrides them and a change here needs a restart.
#
# ⭐ TWO TIERS, TWO SETS OF NAMES, AND CONFUSING THEM IS THE FAILURE THIS SECTION EXISTS TO
# PREVENT. CONTENT_URI / CONTENT_BUCKET above are the EDGE — the browser-visible bucket that
# presigned URLs point at, MinIO in a self-hosted stack. The DURABLE tier is a second, hidden
# bucket that content is mirrored INTO and served back OUT of when the edge has lost it. They
# are different credentials against different providers, and the durable names are the ones a
# cloud deployment actually sets.
#
# ⚠ THE DURABLE TIER IS ON WHEN CONTENT_DURABLE_BUCKET IS SET AND ITS CLIENT BUILDS, AND OFF
# OTHERWISE — SILENTLY. There is no error for a half-configured durable tier: a bucket named
# with credentials that do not work, or credentials set under a misspelled name, leaves a node
# that mirrors nothing while every write succeeds and every read is served from the edge. What
# it costs is not visible until the edge loses an object. `/status` reports the effective
# topology (`minio-only`, `minio-s3-backed`, `cloudfront-s3`) — check it after configuring,
# because that string is the only place the answer appears.
#
# ── the edge ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bucket and credentials. `CONTENT_EDGE_BUCKET` overrides CONTENT_BUCKET above; the two
# CONTENT_ROOT_* names are the fallback a MinIO stack already has set.
#CONTENT_EDGE_BUCKET=
#CONTENT_EDGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
#CONTENT_EDGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
#CONTENT_ROOT_USER=
#CONTENT_ROOT_PASSWORD=
#MINIO_ROOT_USER=
#CONTENT_EDGE_REGION=us-east-1

# A CDN in front of the content bucket's READ path — objects are fetched from this base instead
# of the bucket, and a miss falls back to the bucket. Unset means read from the bucket directly.
#CONTENT_READ_URL_BASE=

# THREE endpoints, because three networks may reach the same bucket by different names: the one
# this process dials, the one a browser is handed in a presigned URL, and the one an MCP server
# in another network context is handed. Each falls back to the AWS_* name beside it and then to
# the internal one, so a stack where all three are the same address sets one value.
#CONTENT_EDGE_ENDPOINT_URL_INTERNAL=
#CONTENT_EDGE_ENDPOINT_URL_PUBLIC=
#CONTENT_EDGE_ENDPOINT_URL=
#AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_INTERNAL=
#AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_PUBLIC=
#AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=

# ── the durable tier ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The bucket is the switch: unset means this node keeps content on the edge alone, which is a
# supported configuration and not a degraded one.
#CONTENT_DURABLE_BUCKET=

# Credentials. Each accepts a CONTENT_DURABLE_AWS_* spelling as well, for deployments whose
# secret store already names them that way; the shorter name wins when both are set.
#CONTENT_DURABLE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
#CONTENT_DURABLE_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
#CONTENT_DURABLE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
#CONTENT_DURABLE_AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Region and endpoint. The region default is `ca-central-1` — an in-code default, not a
# recommendation; a bucket in another region needs this set or every durable call fails.
# Leave the endpoint unset for AWS S3 itself; set it for R2 or any other S3-compatible provider.
#CONTENT_DURABLE_REGION=ca-central-1
#CONTENT_DURABLE_AWS_REGION=
#CONTENT_DURABLE_ENDPOINT_URL=
#CONTENT_DURABLE_AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=

# ⚠ DELETE FROM THE EDGE ONCE THE DURABLE COPY LANDS. Off by default, and turning it on makes
# the durable tier the ONLY copy: every read of an evicted object is a restore from durable
# storage first, so a durable tier that is misconfigured or unreachable stops being an invisible
# omission and starts being unreadable content. Turn it on only after `/status` says
# `minio-s3-backed` and a restore has been observed to work.
#CONTENT_EDGE_EVICT_AFTER_DURABLE_SYNC=false

# Free-space floor for the local content cache, in GB. 0 (the default) is unbounded — the
# warehouse-node behavior. A lean node (a Pi, a workstation) sets this so a growing corpus can
# never take the volume to zero. The deprecated `EMBER_CACHE_MIN_FREE_GB` is still honored, with
# a warning, so a rename does not silently drop a floor somebody is relying on.
#MANTLE_CACHE_MIN_FREE_GB=0


# ═══ Process shape and the change feed ═══════════════════════════════════════
# Read outside `config.py`: `main.py`, `event_backplane.py`, `event_bus.py`.

# Worker processes. Read by `main.py` as a STRING and checked against the back-plane below — it
# does not itself start workers; `mantle-serve --workers N` or the container command does. It is
# named here because the check refuses to boot without it agreeing with reality.
#MANTLE_WORKERS=1

# ⚠ MORE THAN ONE WORKER REQUIRES A BACK-PLANE, AND THE APP REFUSES THE COMBINATION AT BOOT.
# The event bus is in-process: with N workers and no back-plane an event emitted in one worker
# never reaches a subscriber attached to another, so the feed silently loses ~(N-1)/N of its
# deliveries while every worker reports healthy. Unset/`none` is the standalone configuration and
# is complete on its own — one worker needs nothing here.
# Kinds: redis, mqtt (or none). A kind with no URI is refused rather than started half-configured.
#MANTLE_BACKPLANE_KIND=none
#MANTLE_BACKPLANE_URI=

# Bound on a live subscription's queue. A bound rather than none: unbounded does not remove
# backpressure, it relocates it into memory where it is unobservable until the process dies.
#MANTLE_EVENTS_QUEUE_MAX=1000

# ── what a WebSocket subscriber is allowed to keep believing ─────────────────
# A socket outlives the request that opened it, so every verdict it caches is a revocation that
# has not landed yet. These are the clocks that make it land. Raising them buys fewer
# authorization walks on a busy feed and pays for it in how long a revoked principal keeps
# reading; `routers/events_router.py` states each one's reasoning where it is read.

# How long one read verdict stands before the store is asked again. Refusals and permissions
# both expire — caching only the refusals would let a socket learn about grants and never about
# revokes, which is exactly the asymmetry to avoid.
#MANTLE_EVENTS_ACL_TTL_SECONDS=30

# How long the socket's authentication stands before the presented token is resolved again. A
# socket whose token no longer resolves is closed.
#MANTLE_EVENTS_AUTH_TTL_SECONDS=60

# How long a refusal about a principal's OWN brand-new artifact is treated as provisional, and
# how often it is re-asked inside that window. Creating an artifact writes the row and the owner
# grant in two transactions and announces itself between them, so a verdict taken at that instant
# describes a state no reader ever observes. Setting the grace to 0 makes a creator's feed drop
# its own creations; `check_access` still decides, so raising it grants nothing.
#MANTLE_EVENTS_CREATION_GRACE_SECONDS=2.0
#MANTLE_EVENTS_CREATION_RECHECK_SECONDS=0.05

# Ceiling on how many log rows ONE replay pass reads in total. Past it the client is told where
# replay stopped and resumes from there. The bound is what keeps "bounded per call" true: a
# consumer resuming from an empty cursor against a long log would otherwise drain all of it in
# one pass, holding the send lock and the store throughout.
#MANTLE_EVENTS_REPLAY_MAX=5000


# ═══ Indexing behaviour ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Re-index the whole corpus at boot. OFF, and it is off for a measured reason: under S3 write
# contention a full pass is paced by `ClientError (OperationAborted)` rather than by indexing
# work, and a pass that cannot finish between restarts restarts from zero every time. A full
# rebuild is a decision someone makes and schedules — `reindex_all_artifacts()` stays callable
# directly. A node that has never been seeded still indexes on boot regardless of this setting.
#MANTLE_REINDEX_ON_BOOT=

# Leave newly written artifacts LATENT and index them on first access, instead of on write. OFF
# (eager) by default. A per-write `index: "lazy" | "eager"` hint outranks it, so this is the
# deployment default rather than a rule.
#MANTLE_LAZY_INDEX=


# ═══ The image ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Where `/version` reads the build stamp from. The image writes `/app/build_info.json` at build
# time and that is the default; a source checkout falls back to `build_info.json` beside the
# package, and a node with neither reports an unknown version rather than failing.
#BUILD_INFO_PATH=/app/build_info.json


# ═══ Audit and key custody ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Age bound on the access-audit trail, in days. 0 (the default) means unlimited; a malformed or
# negative value also reads as unlimited, because the failure mode of guessing is deleting an
# audit trail nobody asked to have deleted.
#MANTLE_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=0

# Where the KEY-ENCRYPTION KEY lives — the key the search index's per-cell keys are wrapped
# under. `local` (the default) wraps them with this node's own `encryption.key` from KEYS_DIR;
# `kms` and `vault` hand the wrap/unwrap to an external custodian, so the KEK never exists in
# this process and a compromised node cannot unwrap yesterday's cells offline.
#
# ⚠ A PROVIDER THAT CANNOT BE CONSTRUCTED IS A 503, NOT A FALLBACK. `build_key_provider` raises
# rather than quietly reverting to `local` — a search surface that answered by dropping to a
# weaker custody model would be the one failure this setting exists to make impossible. Wrong
# name, missing token, unreachable Vault: no search, and the reason is logged.
#
# ⚠ THE PROVIDER IS NOT INTERCHANGEABLE AFTER CELLS EXIST. Cells are wrapped under whichever
# KEK was live when they were written, so switching providers strands them — the ciphertext is
# intact and nothing on this node can unwrap it. Choose once, at install.
#MANTLE_KEK_PROVIDER=local

# `kms`: the CMK the wrap is delegated to. AWS credentials come from the ambient chain (role,
# instance profile, environment) — there is no separate credential setting here.
#MANTLE_KMS_KEY_ID=

# `vault`: the Transit engine's address, token and key name. All three are required together;
# any one missing is refused at construction rather than at the first search.
#VAULT_ADDR=
#VAULT_TOKEN=
#MANTLE_VAULT_TRANSIT_KEY=


# ═══ Rate limiting ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Per-client sliding window over the whole surface, including API-key verification, so it is the
# floor under credential-stuffing. 0 disables it — which is what `docker-compose.yml` sets, because
# all traffic in a local stack shares one source IP and the limiter is pure noise there.
#AGIENCE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=600

# ⚠ WHO THE LIMITER COUNTS, AND THE ONLY WAY `X-Forwarded-For` IS EVER READ. The client is the
# SOCKET PEER unless the socket peer is named here, as addresses or CIDR blocks, comma-separated.
# The header is written by whoever is calling, so honouring it from any peer hands every caller a
# private window for the asking: a different value per request is a different window, and the
# limit above never applies to anyone who varies it.
#
# UNSET — the default — MEANS TAKE NOBODY'S WORD. A node with no edge in front of it must not be
# talked out of the address it can actually see.
#
# ⚠ BEHIND AN EDGE, SET THIS, and understand what happens if you do not: every request arrives
# from the edge's one address, so all traffic shares ONE window and the limiter starts refusing
# real callers at 600/min in total. That failure is deliberate — an unlisted edge over-counts and
# refuses, rather than under-counting and admitting a flood — but it is a visible outage, so it is
# named here rather than discovered.
#
# The client is taken as the RIGHT-MOST address in the header that is not itself listed here. A
# proxy APPENDS, so anything the caller pre-set stays to the left of what the edge observed; the
# left-most entry is the caller's own claim about itself and is never used.
#
#     AGIENCE_TRUSTED_PROXIES=10.0.0.1,172.16.0.0/12
#
#AGIENCE_TRUSTED_PROXIES=


# ═══ What this file deliberately leaves out ══════════════════════════════════
# `src/mantle/**` reads more environment variables than are named above, and the difference is
# curation rather than oversight. Named here so the next machine-diff of the two lists resolves
# to a decision instead of a discovery.
#
#   EMBER_*         The mesh/shard subtree (`mesh/`, `shard/`) — a peering plane the service
#                   image does not start. `MANTLE_CACHE_MIN_FREE_GB` above is the one that
#                   crossed over, and its EMBER_ spelling is documented there as deprecated.
#   LICENSING_*     `scripts/review_installation.py` and `scripts/generate_usage_snapshot.py`
#   OPHAN_MCP_URI   — operator-run CLI tools, not the service. A `.env` is the wrong place for
#   AGIENCE_OPERATOR_TOKEN  arguments to a command someone types.
#   CLOUDFRONT_*    The signed-CDN read path in front of the durable tier. Whether it is on is
#                   read from the environment, but the private key belongs in a secret store
#                   rather than in a file whose whole purpose is to be copied and edited.
#   EMBEDDINGS_*    The provider facade for an arm that produces no vectors — Mantle never
#                   embeds. The model id labels vectors already at rest, and changing it
#                   orphans their provenance; that is a migration, not a setting.
#   GMAIL_OAUTH_*   Seed provisioning's outbound mail, configured per deployment where the
#   EMAIL_PROVIDER  seeds are, alongside the seed data itself.
#   PLATFORM_EMAIL_ADDRESS
#   LEADS_COLLECTION_SLUG
#   AGIENCE_SEEDS_ROOT      Paths a bootstrap or a test points at, defaulted from the package.
#   AGIENCE_TYPES_PATHS
#
# Everything else `src/mantle/**` reads is above.


# ═══ Test-suite escape hatch ═════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Set to 1 to stop `config.load_env` reading a `.env` at all. The suite sets it so a developer's
# real `.env` can never leak into a run: `load_dotenv` mutates `os.environ` outside monkeypatch's
# restore, so a value that leaked in would persist past the test that caused it.
#AGIENCE_NO_DOTENV=
